2026-06-09  Ivan S. Titov  <iohann.s.titov@gmail.com>

	2.0.4 — "Groundhog Day"

	Idempotence patch.  A flag-by-flag shakeout against a COPY of a real
	/etc/portage — apply, re-apply, compare — caught two ways a mutating
	run could silently misbehave: one that changed nothing when it should
	have, and one that re-changed the file on every run.  Both looked like
	success.  A new property-invariant test tier now enforces, for the
	mutating flags, that a second run is a no-op and that no rewrite
	duplicates a flag or drops an atom.

	* diff_ask (every applying flag): the interactive "Apply changes?"
	  step discarded the pending change on EOF — non-interactive stdin (a
	  pipe, a script, a wrapper) fell out of the read loop and rm'd the
	  temp file with a cheerful "[ ok ]" and no apply.  This was the
	  "portconf -uf changes nothing" report.  EOF now prints "No answer
	  read ... re-run with -y" and returns 1.  Separately the "Yes" branch
	  applied only as root ("go away" + exit for anyone else) while -y did
	  an unchecked mv with no UID check; both paths now simply attempt the
	  write and, on failure, report "Could not write <file> (need root?)"
	  — applying on any writable tree, diagnosing the root-owned
	  /etc/portage case clearly.
	* invalid_uses (-ui/-uf/-f): the agrep "did you mean" correction
	  rewrote each invalid USE flag to a similar valid one and wrote it in.
	  On a real config it mis-mapped genuinely-removed flags to unrelated
	  valid ones and, when the target was already present, duplicated it
	  (mesa gles1/gles2 -> "test ... test"; mpd pipe -> "zip zip"; ffmpeg
	  libdrm -> "libaom ... libaom") — corrupting package.use and making
	  -ui/-uf non-idempotent.  The eix lookup was right (those flags really
	  aren't in current IUSE); only the correction was wrong.  -ui REMOVES
	  invalid flags per the man page, so the agrep loop and prepare_similar()
	  are gone — an invalid flag is simply removed.
	* backup (every mutating flag): etc_update lacked a `:-0` default, so
	  on an empty PORT_ETC timestamp emitted nothing, the `(( ... > ... ))`
	  staleness test raised an arithmetic syntax error, fell through to
	  "already up-to-date", and silently skipped the backup.  Defaulted to
	  0, mirroring portconf_update.
	* current_profile (-cpu): an unguarded readlink on a missing
	  make.profile symlink aborted under set -e + pipefail (unconfigured or
	  sandboxed tree).  Detect the missing symlink up front and print "No
	  active profile" with exit 0.

	Tests:
	* New tests/property/ tier + `make check-properties`: drives the built
	  binary over synthetic, anonymous fixtures and asserts oracle-free
	  invariants — idempotence, no-duplicate-flag, inventory-preservation —
	  for -us/-s/-ku/-ko/-c/-ac and the -ui regression end-to-end.  This is
	  the class the unit/integration suites missed: they stub agrep to a
	  no-op and encode a fixed expected output.  Wired into CI after the
	  smoke tier.  Real-config verification lives in the gitignored
	  scripts/verify-local.sh — same invariants, data never committed.
	* tests/unit/diff_ask.bats: writable-applies / unwritable-reports /
	  EOF-fails-loudly replace the UID-gated cases.
	* tests/unit/backup.bats + tests/unit/profile.bats: empty-PORT_ETC and
	  missing-symlink regressions.
	* tests/integration/invalid_uses.bats: a bounded agrep "match" must not
	  be applied.

	Build:
	* configure.ac: bump to 2.0.4.
	* man/portconf.1: refresh .TH to 2.0.4.

2026-06-09  Ivan S. Titov  <iohann.s.titov@gmail.com>

	2.0.3 — "Safe Word"

	Robustness patch.  A 2>/dev/null audit plus a throwaway-sandbox
	shakeout of every flag (solo, combined, and against poisoned
	fixtures) surfaced a class of aborts under set -euo pipefail: a
	command whose non-zero exit is hidden by 2>/dev/null, an unbound
	variable that `|| true` can't catch, or a function whose
	last-command exit leaks to its bare caller.  All hit edge cases —
	non-root, degenerate input, multi-flag runs — and none change the
	normal-path behaviour.

	* eix_check: any non-root eix-dependent run (e.g. `-p -ui`)
	  aborted right after "Creating new temporary cache." — the
	  temp-cache `chown portage:portage` fails for non-root and
	  2>/dev/null hid the message but not the exit.  Only root (CI)
	  escaped it, so it read as environmental.  Guarded with `|| true`;
	  non-root smoke 27/34 -> 34/34.
	* dispatch: multi-flag runs (e.g. `-s -t -uf -ku -sm -sum`)
	  aborted — the eix_cache cleanup referenced an unset variable once
	  a prior arm had unset it (set -u).  Now `${eix_cache:-}`.
	* invalid_uses: `-ui`/`-uf`/`-f` on an empty or comments-only
	  package.use aborted on an unset `atoms`.  Reset per file (file
	  and directory layouts).
	* invalid_uses_make + use_makeconf: `-ui`/`-um`/`-uf`/`-f` with no
	  make.conf ran `cp ""` and aborted.  Both return early when
	  make.conf is absent.
	* not_if / installed: `-ft` aborted twice — a not-installed atom
	  failed the qlist pipeline under pipefail, and the function's
	  trailing test leaked a false exit to its bare `installed;`
	  caller.  `|| true` on the pipeline plus an explicit `return 0`.
	* restore / startup / overlays / mask_trash / invalid_uses /
	  not_found: the remaining 2>/dev/null sites (tar extract,
	  make.conf source, repos.conf cats, four `sed -i`) guarded so a
	  non-zero exit lets `eend` report the status instead of aborting.

	Build:
	* configure.ac: bump to 2.0.3.
	* man/portconf.1: refresh .TH to 2.0.3 (was stale at 2.0.0 — never
	  bumped for 2.0.1/2.0.2).
	* .gitignore: stop tracking generated packaging/Makefile{,.in},
	  matching src/, man/, completion/.

2026-06-01  Ivan S. Titov  <iohann.s.titov@gmail.com>

	2.0.2 — "Comment Section"

	Patch release driven by a pollution-test follow-up on 2.0.1.
	Real /etc/portage exercised against a synthetic fixture surfaced
	four user-visible behaviours that worked-by-accident in 2.0.1.

	* sort_uses (in -s/-us/-uf/-f) destroyed every header comment
	  above each atom — the same comment-loss class as sort_uniq_files
	  (both fixed here; 2.0.1 had only fixed sort_uniq_files's separate
	  prompt-dropping bug).
	* sort_keys + sort_keywords (in -s/-ku/-ko/-f) destroyed the same
	  class of comments in package.keywords / package.accept_keywords.
	* sort_uniq_mask_use (in -s/-sup/-uf/-f) destroyed comments in
	  profile/use.mask / use.stable.mask.
	* invalid_uses (in -ui/-uf/-f) silently rewrote typos to absurd
	  matches: `qemu jit` got substituted with `qemu accessibility`
	  because agrep's unanchored fuzzy search matched the embedded
	  `ity` substring inside accessibility at edit distance 1.  And
	  when invalid_uses stripped all USE flags from an atom, the
	  comment block above the (now bare) atom was left stranded.

	Source-side fixes:
	* src/portconf.in (sort_uses, sort_uniq_files): single gawk pass
	  groups each atom with the consecutive comment + blank lines
	  immediately above it ("header block"); blocks travel with
	  their atom on sort.  Trailing comments emit verbatim at EOF.
	  Inline comments still survive.  sort_uniq_files keeps bare
	  atoms (package.mask use case); sort_uses drops them.
	* src/portconf.in (sort_keys, sort_keywords): same approach for
	  the keyword pair.  sort_keys does per-base last-state-wins
	  (~ and - prefix-stripped for base detection); sort_keywords
	  keeps the absolute last token per atom.  Both default to
	  ~$ARCH when empty.
	* src/portconf.in (sort_uniq_mask_use): per-token comment-
	  preserving rewrite for the flat-list use.mask / use.stable.mask
	  case.  Each flag carries the consecutive comment + blank
	  lines above it as its header.  Ordering uses a monotonic
	  token counter — no float-precision games, no ties when a base
	  is re-encountered.
	* src/portconf.in (invalid_uses agrep): the typo-suggestion
	  fuzzy match now anchors to ^${use}$ (full-line) so a 3-char
	  typo can't match a 13-char IUSE via substring.  Edit distance
	  hard-capped at 3; the `-B` (best-match-regardless) fallback is
	  dropped.  Anything beyond 3 edits falls through to
	  incorrect_use and gets removed as truly invalid.
	* src/portconf.in (invalid_uses bare-atom drop): when stripping
	  flags leaves an atom bare, the line is removed AND its
	  preceding header block goes too — no more orphaned comments.
	  Two sed-driven loops collapsed into one awk cleanup pass.
	  Per-atom "Removing atom: X" messages emit in a pre-loop scan
	  (anchored to exclude single-token comment lines) so the UI
	  shape is unchanged.
	* src/portconf.in (startup): gawk runtime probe.  Both sort_*
	  rewrites use `asorti()` which is gawk-only; non-gawk awk
	  installs (busybox, mawk, eselect-overridden) now fail loudly
	  at script start with an `emerge dev-lang/gawk && eselect awk
	  set gawk` hint, not mid-mutation with an opaque error.
	* src/portconf.in (not_found): the first `eix -Ttc` was unguarded
	  under set -e while its sibling already had `|| true`.  eix's test
	  mode exits non-zero when it finds entries, so portconf -t/-f/-fr
	  aborted at not_found whenever there was something to clean.
	  Guarded it to match the sibling.

	Tests:
	* tests/unit/sort_uniq_files.bats: 5 new comment-preservation
	  cases (header travel on sort, multi-line headers, blank-line-
	  in-header, trailing EOF comments, duplicate-atom header).
	* tests/unit/sort_uses.bats: 8 new (header travel + multi-line +
	  trailing + bare-atom-drops-its-header + 3 edge cases:
	  header+inline combo, divider-between-atoms, `atom #notext`).
	* tests/unit/sort_keywords.bats, uniq_keywords.bats: header-
	  preservation + travel + EOF trailing.
	* tests/unit/sort_uniq_mask_use.bats: header-preservation + EOF
	  trailing + re-asserted-flag ordering regression.
	* tests/integration/invalid_uses.bats: 3 new — agrep-B fallback
	  regression, bare-atom-drop also strips header, surrounding
	  atoms keep their headers.
	* tests/smoke/mutating_flags.bats: end-to-end -f -y regression
	  asserting both header lines of a two-line comment block above
	  a directory-layout package.use atom survive the full dispatch
	  chain.

	Build:
	* configure.ac: bump to 2.0.2.

2026-05-31  Ivan S. Titov  <iohann.s.titov@gmail.com>

	2.0.1 — "Read From Behind"

	Patch release for one real bug + a post-audit accuracy pass on the
	in-tree comments and shipped docs.

	The bug: every interactive Yes/No prompt in diff_ask / remove_ask
	was silently dropping the user's answer when called from inside an
	outer `done < <(find ...)` loop — sort_uniq_files, file_or_dir's
	directory branch, force_not_installed, empty_files, backup_files.
	Net effect on a modern Gentoo host where /etc/portage/package.* is
	a directory of fragment files: every diff_ask in the -s / -us /
	-uf / -t arms looked normal, but the planned mutation was silently
	thrown away.  -y users were unaffected (the auto-apply branch
	bypasses the read).  Smoke tier didn't catch it because every
	smoke test ran with -p.

	Source-side fixes:
	* src/portconf.in (diff_ask, remove_ask, dispatch entry):
	  prompt reads now come from fd 9 — a dup of the dispatch-entry
	  stdin established once via `exec 9<&0` before any loop body
	  runs.  fd 9 specifically because bats reserves fd 3 for its
	  own output channel; a fd-3 dup would make the unit tests read
	  bats's stream instead of the heredoc-fed stdin.  Falls back to
	  fd 0 when fd 9 isn't open (PORTCONF_NO_MAIN sourcing).
	* src/portconf.in: PORTDIR default migrated from the 2014-era
	  /usr/portage to the modern /var/db/repos/gentoo.  Only fires
	  when neither env nor make.conf sets PORTDIR (i.e. chroot,
	  test-harness, minimal-host fallback) — every realistic Gentoo
	  install sets PORTDIR via make.conf and is unaffected.

	Tests:
	* tests/smoke/mutating_flags.bats: new regression — set up
	  package.use as a directory, run -us without -y while feeding
	  "Yes" on stdin, assert the fragment was normalised.  Skipped
	  on non-root hosts (diff_ask's Yes branch gates mv on UID==0).
	  Without the fix, the file would stay untouched and the test
	  would fail.

	Accuracy pass on docstrings + section banners (comment-only,
	behaviour unchanged):
	* src/portconf.in: 17 docstring inaccuracies fixed, in five
	  classes — three direction inversions (not_if, installed,
	  use_makeconf), three description rotations (package_env_conf
	  / package_env / env_not_installed), one outright-wrong subject
	  (hell_parser claimed to parse eix IUSE fields; actually parses
	  any NAME="..." shell assignment), and ten cases of factual
	  drift (wrong filename, wrong scope, stale flag names, omitted
	  branches).
	* src/portconf.in: four section-banner copy errors fixed —
	  PROFILE MASK USE (use.force → use.stable.mask), WORLD REBUILD
	  (cited the wrong primary source command), INIT ("All source-
	  time effects" was misleading: _compute_ignore is a function
	  def), make.conf USE SYNC (renamed to make.conf USE PRUNE so
	  banner direction matches function direction).
	* src/portconf.in: package_envs() relocated from VERSION COMPARE
	  to MASK / UNMASK CLEANUP — the latter banner named it, the
	  former contained it.  No caller change (bash function lookup
	  is name-based, not source-order).

	Docs:
	* SECURITY.md: the `qatom -F re-parse drift` in-scope entry cited
	  two SHAs (`dcd0265` and `3fc5391`) that don't resolve in the
	  repo — carried forward from the HIGH#1 commit body's narrative
	  without re-validation.  Replaced with the real anchors:
	  7e5ccd7 (package_envs modernised for qatom 0.97+) and 0bf7162
	  (HIGH#1 sed-injection + file_or_dir diagnostic cascade).
	* README.md: corrected user-facing counts — 281 tests (179 unit
	  + 70 integration + 32 smoke) was reported as 280; 31 flags /
	  9 categories was reported as 35 / 8.

	Build:
	* configure.ac: bump to 2.0.1.

